## 🎯 Day 34 Objective

Most AI governance programs die in the gap between *policy* and *practice*. A team writes a beautiful ISO 42001 / NIST AI RMF policy, then has no operational artifact to actually run it. The **AI Risk Register** is that artifact — the single source of truth where every AI system, its risks, owners, controls, and residual exposure live and get reviewed.

Today I built a **ready-to-use AI Risk Register template** — the taxonomy, the scoring model, the workflow, and three worked examples — so a governance lead can stand one up in an afternoon instead of a quarter.

---

## 🔍 What I Did

- Defined an **AI-specific risk taxonomy** (not a generic infosec register with "AI" bolted on)
- Built a **likelihood × impact scoring model** with explicit anchors so scores are repeatable across reviewers
- Designed the **register schema** (the columns) and the **lifecycle workflow** (how a risk moves from identified → treated → monitored)
- Mapped every risk category back to **ISO 42001, NIST AI RMF, and EU AI Act** so the register doubles as audit evidence
- Wrote **3 worked example entries** (a RAG chatbot, an HR screening model, a third-party GenAI connector) to show the template in real use

