## 🧭 The AI Risk Taxonomy

A good register starts with a shared vocabulary. Generic risk registers fail on AI because they can't name model-specific failure modes. These eight categories cover the AI lifecycle:

| # | Risk Category | What It Captures | Example Risk |
|---|---------------|------------------|--------------|
| 1 | **Data & Privacy** | Training/inference data quality, provenance, PII exposure, consent, retention | Model memorizes and regurgitates PII from training data |
| 2 | **Bias & Fairness** | Disparate outcomes across protected groups, proxy discrimination | Screening model down-ranks candidates from one demographic |
| 3 | **Robustness & Reliability** | Model drift, hallucination, adversarial inputs, degradation over time | Accuracy silently decays 6 months post-deployment |
| 4 | **Transparency & Explainability** | Inability to explain a decision, missing model documentation | Regulator asks "why was this loan denied" and no one can answer |
| 5 | **Security** | Prompt injection, model/data exfiltration, poisoning, insecure plugins | Injected prompt causes the agent to leak system context |
| 6 | **Human Oversight & Autonomy** | Over-reliance, missing human-in-the-loop for high-stakes decisions | Automated action taken with no human review path |
| 7 | **Legal & Regulatory** | Non-compliance with EU AI Act, sector law, IP/copyright of outputs | High-risk system deployed without conformity assessment |
| 8 | **Third-Party & Supply Chain** | Vendor model risk, dataset licensing, connector/API exposure | Foundation-model vendor changes behavior without notice |

